<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Vermandois</h1><p class="st-meta">896 CE – 1213 CE (approx.) · capital Saint-Quentin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Picard county held by descendants of Charlemagne, whose counts were king-makers in 10th-century France before the land returned to the crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Herbert I, a Carolingian descended from Bernard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, became count of Vermandois around 896, with his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a>. His family built up lands across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-champagne">Champagne</a> during the struggles between the Carolingian kings and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-ii-count-of-vermandois">Herbert II</a> was the most powerful lord of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 920s, holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">King Charles the Simple</a> prisoner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronne">Péronne</a> until the king's death in 929 and seizing the archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> for his young son. Later counts were less prominent. Around 1080 the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-countess-of-vermandois">Adelaide</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-vermandois">Hugh of Vermandois</a>, brother of King Philip I and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 12th century the county passed by marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-flanders">Philip of Alsace</a>, count of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a>, who lost much of it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-boves">Treaty of Boves</a> in 1185. After the death of Countess Eleanor in 1213 the rest was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-vermandois.jpg" alt="County of Vermandois" width="320" height="307"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (sidonius 12:09, 2 May 2008 (UTC)) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burgundian_northern_France_1465%E2%80%931477.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>896 CE – 1213 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Quentin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Herbert II of Vermandois, Hugh of Vermandois, Raoul I of Vermandois</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vermandois</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1054&amp;focus=county-of-vermandois" aria-label="County of Vermandois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4755 443 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Vermandois lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5075 699L5054 728M5075 699L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5075 699h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5075 699h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1054&amp;focus=county-of-vermandois">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
